IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ofek-domain`

§ `The working week, the post, and the value of an hour`

-- ===========================================================================
-- §§ 14 to 33 of the collective agreement of 25 December 2008.
--
-- Ofek Hadash's central bargain was structural, not just numerical: it bought
-- a longer, differently-shaped week. A full post became 36 hours SPENT IN THE
-- SCHOOL, split three ways — hours in front of a class, hours with a small
-- group or a single pupil, and hours present in the building doing the work
-- that teaching requires. That three-way split is what this module encodes,
-- because everything paid by the hour is priced off it (§ 31).
--
-- THE THREE KINDS OF HOUR, in the agreement's own vocabulary:
--   frontal    (sha'ot frontaliyot)  — in front of a class
--   individual (sha'ot pratani'ot)   — with up to 5 pupils, or one pupil
--   presence   (sha'ot shehiya)      — in the school: meetings, preparation,
--                                      marking, meeting parents
-- ===========================================================================

§§ `Section 14 — the working days`

-- § 14: a full-post teacher works five days a week, INCLUDING in schools that
-- operate six days. The four-day arrangement is a grandfathered exception,
-- needing the principal's agreement, and capped at a nine-hour day.
`the working days in a full week` MEANS 5
`the longest day allowed under the four-day exception` MEANS 9

GIVEN `the teacher worked four days before the crossover` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the school had already moved to a five-day week`   IS A BOOLEAN
      `the principal agrees`                              IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the teacher may keep working four days` IF
        `the teacher worked four days before the crossover`
    AND `the school had already moved to a five-day week`
    AND `the principal agrees`

§§ `Sections 15 and 17 — the shape of the week`

DECLARE `a weekly work pattern` HAS
    `frontal hours`    IS A NUMBER
    `individual hours` IS A NUMBER
    `presence hours`   IS A NUMBER

GIVEN `the pattern` IS A `a weekly work pattern`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the hours in` `the pattern`
    @nlg the total weekly hours in %the pattern%
    MEANS   `the pattern`'s `frontal hours`
      PLUS  `the pattern`'s `individual hours`
      PLUS  `the pattern`'s `presence hours`

-- § 15(a)-(d) and § 17(a)-(d): the ordinary full-post week, by stage.
GIVEN `the stage` IS A `a stage of education`
GIVETH A `a weekly work pattern`
`the ordinary full-post week in` `the stage`
    @nlg the ordinary full-post week in %the stage%
    MEANS
    -- § 15 gives the primary school 36 hours as 26 frontal, 5 individual and
    -- 5 presence, of which up to two are working meetings. § 17 gives the
    -- junior high the same 36, cut 23/4/9 with up to four meeting hours: it
    -- gives up three frontal hours to presence, which is the reform's answer
    -- to the older pupils. Built positionally, so the two rows line up and the
    -- trade is visible as a column.
    --                                                     frontal, individual, presence
    CONSIDER `the stage`
    WHEN `a primary school`     THEN `a weekly work pattern` OF      26,          5,        5
    WHEN `a junior high school` ^    ^                       ^       23,          4,        9

-- § 15, closing table, and § 17, closing table: a teacher who is also GRADE
-- COORDINATOR (memuneh shichva) keeps the 36 hours but re-cuts them, giving
-- up frontal hours as the grade grows. The agreement prints this as a table
-- with four bands, in both articles.
GIVEN `the stage` IS A `a stage of education`
      `the classes in the grade` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `a weekly work pattern`
`the full-post week of a grade coordinator in` `the stage` `the classes in the grade`
    @nlg the full-post week of a grade coordinator in %the stage%
    MEANS
    -- The tables § 15 and § 17 print at the foot of each article, as tables:
    -- one row per band of grade size, three columns of hours. The total stays
    -- 36 throughout — the coordinator gives up frontal hours, not working time.
    CONSIDER `the stage`
    WHEN `a primary school` THEN
        --                                              frontal, individual, presence
        BRANCH IF `the classes in the grade` AT MOST 4 THEN `a weekly work pattern` OF 26,          4,        6
               IF ^                          AT MOST 6 ^    ^                       ^  25,          3,        8
               IF ^                          AT MOST 8 ^    ^                       ^  24,          3,        9
               OTHERWISE                                    ^                       ^  23,          3,       10
    WHEN `a junior high school` THEN
        --                                              frontal, individual, presence
        BRANCH IF `the classes in the grade` AT MOST 4 THEN `a weekly work pattern` OF 23,          3,       10
               IF ^                          AT MOST 6 ^    ^                       ^  22,          2,       12
               IF ^                          AT MOST 8 ^    ^                       ^  21,          2,       13
               OTHERWISE                                    ^                       ^  20,          2,       14

§§ `Section 26 — the age hours`

-- § 26 gives older teachers back some of the week, and it draws the line
-- differently for existing and new teachers: an existing teacher gets a
-- second reduction at 55, a new teacher does not (§ 26(c), final sentence).
-- § 26(d) fixes entitlement for the whole school year by the teacher's age on
-- 31 December falling in it.
GIVEN `the teacher` IS A `a teacher`
      `the age on 31 December of the school year` IS A NUMBER
      `the pattern` IS A `a weekly work pattern`
GIVETH A `a weekly work pattern`
`the week after the age reduction for` `the teacher` `the age on 31 December of the school year` `the pattern`
    @nlg the weekly pattern of %the teacher% after the section 26 age reduction
    MEANS
    CONSIDER `the teacher`'s `how they entered`
    WHEN `an existing teacher converted under sections 43 to 50` THEN
        -- § 26(a): 50 up to but not including 55 — two frontal hours off.
        -- § 26(b): 55 and above — three frontal hours and one individual.
        BRANCH IF `the age on 31 December of the school year` AT LEAST 55 THEN `a weekly work pattern` WITH
                   `frontal hours`    IS `the pattern`'s `frontal hours` MINUS 3
                   `individual hours` IS `the pattern`'s `individual hours` MINUS 1
                   `presence hours`   IS `the pattern`'s `presence hours`
               IF `the age on 31 December of the school year` AT LEAST 50 THEN `a weekly work pattern` WITH
                   `frontal hours`    IS `the pattern`'s `frontal hours` MINUS 2
                   `individual hours` IS `the pattern`'s `individual hours`
                   `presence hours`   IS `the pattern`'s `presence hours`
               OTHERWISE `the pattern`
    WHEN `a new teacher placed under section 36` THEN
        -- § 26(c): 50 and above — two frontal hours off, and nothing further
        -- at 55.
        IF `the age on 31 December of the school year` AT LEAST 50
            THEN `a weekly work pattern` WITH
                     `frontal hours`    IS `the pattern`'s `frontal hours` MINUS 2
                     `individual hours` IS `the pattern`'s `individual hours`
                     `presence hours`   IS `the pattern`'s `presence hours`
            ELSE `the pattern`

§§ `Sections 27 and 33 — how large a post may be`

-- § 27(a): the floor is a third of a post. § 27(c): a NEW teacher may not go
-- below half a post without the approval of the head of teaching personnel,
-- unless § 27(d) or § 27(e) applies to them.
`the smallest post in the reform` MEANS 1 DIVIDED BY 3
`the smallest post for a new teacher without approval` MEANS 50%

GIVEN `the teacher` IS A `a teacher`
      `special approval has been given` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the teacher is paramedical or an integration teacher` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the post is large enough` IF
    -- § 27(e): a paramedical teacher or an integration teacher (mo'ra
    -- meshalevet) may be employed below a third of a post, so the floor does
    -- not reach them at all.
    IF `the teacher is paramedical or an integration teacher`
        THEN TRUE
        ELSE     `the teacher`'s `fraction of a full post` AT LEAST `the smallest post in the reform`
             AND `the new-teacher floor is satisfied`
    WHERE
        `the new-teacher floor is satisfied` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the teacher`'s `how they entered`
            WHEN `a new teacher placed under section 36` THEN
                    `the teacher`'s `fraction of a full post` AT LEAST `the smallest post for a new teacher without approval`
                OR (`special approval has been given` AND `the teacher`'s `teaches special education`)
                OR `special approval has been given`
            WHEN `an existing teacher converted under sections 43 to 50` THEN TRUE

-- § 33(a): the ceiling is a full post. § 33(c) lets the districts go beyond
-- it where a teacher is needed, on conditions.
`the largest ordinary post` MEANS 100%

-- § 33(c)(2): where a teacher is employed beyond a full post, the extra hours
-- are frontal only, the day is capped at 8.5 hours (5 on a Friday), and the
-- week at 42 hours. § 33(b)(3) applies the same 42-hour week to a teacher
-- who also works in a school outside the reform.
`the longest day beyond a full post` MEANS 8.5
`the longest friday beyond a full post` MEANS 5
`the longest week beyond a full post` MEANS 42

GIVEN `the hours in the day` IS A NUMBER
      `the hours in the week` IS A NUMBER
      `the day is a friday` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the section 33 hour caps are respected` IF
        `the hours in the week` AT MOST `the longest week beyond a full post`
    AND (IF `the day is a friday`
            THEN `the hours in the day` AT MOST `the longest friday beyond a full post`
            ELSE `the hours in the day` AT MOST `the longest day beyond a full post`)

§§ `Section 31 — the value of an hour`

-- § 31(b), verbatim: "The basis for calculating the value of an hour for a
-- 100% post: 4.33 (weeks) X 36 (hours)". Both numbers are printed in the
-- article; neither is derived here.
`the weeks in a month for this purpose` MEANS 4.33
`the hours in a full-post week` MEANS 36
`the hours in a full-post month` MEANS `the weeks in a month for this purpose` TIMES `the hours in a full-post week`

-- § 31(a): the article does NOT use one salary base for every hourly payment.
-- It names four purposes and gives each its own base, and the difference
-- matters: a substitution hour is priced off the combined salary alone, while
-- a school activity is priced off the combined salary PLUS the role
-- supplements that count toward the pensionable salary.
DECLARE `a purpose for which an hour is valued` IS ONE OF
    `a substitution hour`                             -- § 31(a)(1)
    `a substitution hour taken in place of a presence hour`  -- § 31(a)(2)
    `a school activity`                               -- § 31(a)(3)
    `a deduction for an hour of absence`              -- § 31(a)(4)
    `an hour worked beyond a full post`               -- § 33(c)(3), applying § 31(a)(1)

GIVEN `the purpose` IS A `a purpose for which an hour is valued`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the role supplements enter the hourly base for` `the purpose` IF
    -- § 31(a)(1) takes the combined salary alone — except that for a special
    -- education teacher substituting in a special education class it also
    -- takes the special education supplement. § 31(a)(2), (3) and (4) all
    -- take the combined salary together with the role supplements counted
    -- toward the pensionable salary.
    CONSIDER `the purpose`
    WHEN `a substitution hour`                                     THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a substitution hour taken in place of a presence hour`   THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a school activity`                                       THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a deduction for an hour of absence`                      THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an hour worked beyond a full post`                       THEN FALSE

GIVEN `the monthly base for the purpose` IS A NUMBER
      `the fraction of a full post` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the value of an hour on a base of` `the monthly base for the purpose` `the fraction of a full post`
    @nlg the value of one hour on a monthly base of %the monthly base for the purpose%
    MEANS
    -- § 31(b) divides by the FULL-POST month, not by the teacher's own hours.
    -- The base handed in is the teacher's actual monthly figure, already
    -- pro-rated for a part post, so the pro-rating is undone here: two
    -- teachers on the same rank and seniority are worth the same per hour
    -- whatever the size of their post.
    IF `the fraction of a full post` AT MOST 0
        THEN 0
        ELSE (`the monthly base for the purpose` DIVIDED BY `the fraction of a full post`)
                 DIVIDED BY `the hours in a full-post month`

§§ `Sections 29 and 30 — the hours paid on top`

-- § 29(b) and § 30(a): both a substitution hour and a school activity are
-- paid at 125% of the value of an hour.
`the rate for a substitution hour` MEANS 125%
`the rate for a school activity` MEANS 125%
`the rate for an ordinary presence hour` MEANS 100%
-- § 33(c)(3): an hour beyond a full post is paid at 100%, not 125%.
`the rate for an hour beyond a full post` MEANS 100%

-- § 30(a): a school activity is capped at 18 hours in each HALF of the school
-- year, and § 30(c) caps each kind within that: a general school assembly at
-- 4 hours, individual parent-and-child meetings at 8, an after-hours event
-- at 6. The three sub-caps sum to 18, so the overall cap binds exactly when
-- every kind is exhausted.
`the school activity hours allowed in a half year` MEANS 18
`the assembly hours allowed in a half year` MEANS 4
`the parent and child meeting hours allowed in a half year` MEANS 8
`the after-hours event hours allowed in a half year` MEANS 6

GIVEN `the assembly hours` IS A NUMBER
      `the parent and child meeting hours` IS A NUMBER
      `the after-hours event hours` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the school activity caps are respected` IF
        `the assembly hours` AT MOST `the assembly hours allowed in a half year`
    AND `the parent and child meeting hours` AT MOST `the parent and child meeting hours allowed in a half year`
    AND `the after-hours event hours` AT MOST `the after-hours event hours allowed in a half year`
    AND (`the assembly hours` PLUS `the parent and child meeting hours` PLUS `the after-hours event hours`)
            AT MOST `the school activity hours allowed in a half year`

-- § 29(c)-(e) and § 30(d)-(f): the four cases the agreement spells out for an
-- hour worked in place of, or beside, a presence hour. The third is the one
-- worth encoding — the teacher who moves the presence hour to another day is
-- paid BOTH, and the one who does not is paid only the 125%.
DECLARE `how the extra hour stood to the presence hour` IS ONE OF
    `it fell on the teacher's free day`                       -- § 29(c), § 30(d)
    `it replaced a presence hour that was not made up`        -- § 29(d), § 30(e)
    `it replaced a presence hour made up on another day`      -- § 29(e), § 30(f)

GIVEN `the case` IS A `how the extra hour stood to the presence hour`
      `the value of an hour` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the pay for one extra hour where` `the case` `the value of an hour`
    @nlg the pay for one extra hour where %the case%
    MEANS
    CONSIDER `the case`
    WHEN `it fell on the teacher's free day` THEN
        `the value of an hour` TIMES `the rate for a substitution hour`
    WHEN `it replaced a presence hour that was not made up` THEN
        -- 125% for the hour worked, and the ordinary 100% for the presence
        -- hour is NOT paid: it was not worked.
        `the value of an hour` TIMES `the rate for a substitution hour`
    WHEN `it replaced a presence hour made up on another day` THEN
        -- 125% for the hour worked AND 100% for the presence hour, which was.
        `the value of an hour` TIMES (`the rate for a substitution hour` PLUS `the rate for an ordinary presence hour`)

§§ `Illustrations`

-- § 31(b): the divisor, on the agreement's own two numbers.
#ASSERT `the hours in a full-post month` EQUALS 155.88

-- A teacher whose monthly base is 10,000 on a full post is worth 10,000 /
-- 155.88 an hour, and a substitution hour pays a quarter more again.
#ASSERT ROUND (`the value of an hour on a base of` 10_000 1 TIMES 100) EQUALS 6415
#ASSERT ROUND (`the value of an hour on a base of` 10_000 1 TIMES `the rate for a substitution hour` TIMES 100) EQUALS 8019

-- The hourly value does not depend on the size of the post: a half-post
-- teacher on 5,000 is worth exactly what a full-post teacher on 10,000 is.
#ASSERT `the value of an hour on a base of` 5_000 (50%) EQUALS `the value of an hour on a base of` 10_000 1

-- § 29(e) and § 30(f): making the presence hour up on another day is worth
-- 225% in total, against 125% for not making it up.
#ASSERT `the pay for one extra hour where` `it replaced a presence hour made up on another day` 100 EQUALS 225
#ASSERT `the pay for one extra hour where` `it replaced a presence hour that was not made up` 100 EQUALS 125
#ASSERT `the pay for one extra hour where` `it fell on the teacher's free day` 100 EQUALS 125

-- THE INVARIANT OF THE REFORM: a full post is 36 hours, whichever stage the
-- teacher is in and however the grade coordinator's week is re-cut. The
-- agreement never states this in terms — it prints eight patterns across two
-- articles and leaves the reader to add them up. Encoding it makes the claim
-- checkable, and it holds in all ten patterns.
#ASSERT `the hours in` (`the ordinary full-post week in` `a primary school`) EQUALS 36
#ASSERT `the hours in` (`the ordinary full-post week in` `a junior high school`) EQUALS 36
#ASSERT `the hours in` (`the full-post week of a grade coordinator in` `a primary school` 3) EQUALS 36
#ASSERT `the hours in` (`the full-post week of a grade coordinator in` `a primary school` 6) EQUALS 36
#ASSERT `the hours in` (`the full-post week of a grade coordinator in` `a primary school` 8) EQUALS 36
#ASSERT `the hours in` (`the full-post week of a grade coordinator in` `a primary school` 12) EQUALS 36
#ASSERT `the hours in` (`the full-post week of a grade coordinator in` `a junior high school` 3) EQUALS 36
#ASSERT `the hours in` (`the full-post week of a grade coordinator in` `a junior high school` 6) EQUALS 36
#ASSERT `the hours in` (`the full-post week of a grade coordinator in` `a junior high school` 8) EQUALS 36
#ASSERT `the hours in` (`the full-post week of a grade coordinator in` `a junior high school` 12) EQUALS 36

-- And the coordinator gives up frontal hours as the grade grows, which is the
-- point of the table.
#ASSERT (`the full-post week of a grade coordinator in` `a junior high school` 12)'s `frontal hours` EQUALS 20
#ASSERT (`the ordinary full-post week in` `a junior high school`)'s `frontal hours` EQUALS 23
#ASSERT (`the ordinary full-post week in` `a primary school`)'s `frontal hours` EQUALS 26

-- § 26: an EXISTING teacher of 56 loses three frontal hours and one
-- individual hour; a NEW teacher of 56 loses two frontal hours and nothing
-- else. That difference is § 26(c)'s closing sentence, and it is worth 1.9%
-- of a week.
#ASSERT `the hours in` (`the week after the age reduction for` `Dvora` 56 (`the ordinary full-post week in` `a primary school`)) EQUALS 32
#ASSERT `the hours in` (`the week after the age reduction for` `Yael` 56 (`the ordinary full-post week in` `a junior high school`)) EQUALS 34
#ASSERT `the hours in` (`the week after the age reduction for` `Dvora` 52 (`the ordinary full-post week in` `a primary school`)) EQUALS 34
#ASSERT `the hours in` (`the week after the age reduction for` `Dvora` 49 (`the ordinary full-post week in` `a primary school`)) EQUALS 36
#ASSERT `the hours in` (`the week after the age reduction for` `Yael` 49 (`the ordinary full-post week in` `a junior high school`)) EQUALS 36

-- § 27: a third of a post is the floor, and a new teacher needs half a post
-- or an approval. Noa is a new teacher on exactly half a post, so she is
-- inside § 27(c) without needing one.
#ASSERT `the post is large enough` `Noa` FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `the post is large enough` `Yael` FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `the post is large enough` `Dvora` FALSE FALSE

-- § 33(c)(2): the caps on a week that runs beyond a full post.
#ASSERT `the section 33 hour caps are respected` 8.5 42 FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `the section 33 hour caps are respected` 9 42 FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `the section 33 hour caps are respected` 8 43 FALSE
#ASSERT `the section 33 hour caps are respected` 5 40 TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `the section 33 hour caps are respected` 6 40 TRUE

-- § 30: the three sub-caps sum exactly to the overall cap, so a teacher who
-- exhausts every kind of activity is at 18 hours and no more.
#ASSERT `the assembly hours allowed in a half year` PLUS `the parent and child meeting hours allowed in a half year` PLUS `the after-hours event hours allowed in a half year` EQUALS `the school activity hours allowed in a half year`
#ASSERT `the school activity caps are respected` 4 8 6
#ASSERT NOT `the school activity caps are respected` 5 8 6
#ASSERT NOT `the school activity caps are respected` 4 9 6

-- § 31(a): the bases differ by purpose, and a substitution hour is the one
-- priced off the combined salary alone.
#ASSERT NOT `the role supplements enter the hourly base for` `a substitution hour`
#ASSERT `the role supplements enter the hourly base for` `a school activity`
#ASSERT `the role supplements enter the hourly base for` `a deduction for an hour of absence`
#ASSERT NOT `the role supplements enter the hourly base for` `an hour worked beyond a full post`
